      "textEn": "8\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nSadhyinaah—otherwise engrossed; Vayam—we; Nunam—indeed; Na—not; Stri—wives; Apatya—children; Artha—for wealth; Kamukhaah—desiring, greedy; Atmalabdhya—being content with oneself; Asmahe—we are living; Purnah—complete; Gehayoh—towards the body and home; Jyotih—like fire; Akriyaah—not engaged in any work. We do not care about women, children, or property at all. Always being content with the self,\nWe do not work for the body and home, but like light, we remain only a witness. Srisuka said:\nHaving thus spoken, the Lord, like a beloved one,... Having analyzed the mind, it is free from pride. 21. Meaning of the word.\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami said; Etatvat—This much; Uktya—By saying; Bhagavan—God; Atmanam—Your own;\nYourself; Vallabham—To beloved; Iva—Like; Manyamanam—By believing; Avishleshat—Without separating from; Tat—That;\nThat; Darap—Of arrogance; Ghnah—Destroyer; Uparamat—Bound.\n.\n\nShukdev Goswami said: Rukmini considered herself the most special beloved of God\nbecause she had never left his company. Saying these things, God removed their pride.\nAnd then stopped speaking. Thus, the lord of the three worlds, then, the self-existent one,\nHaving heard of the deity's beloved, the pleasant, the unheard-of. With tears in her heart, she walks on the path of the wind.\nShe cries in anxiety and distress, and goes to the world. 22). Meaning of the word\n\nThus—in this way; Trilok—of the three worlds; Isha—of the lords; Pateh—of the master; Tada—then; Atmanah—of oneself;\nPriyasya—of the beloved; Devi—of the goddess Rukmini; Ashruta—never heard; Purvam—before; Apriyam—dislike; Ashritya—\nHaving heard; Bhitah—frightened; Hriday—in one's heart; Jata—born; Vepathu—wave; Chintam—worry; Durantam—terrifying;\nRudati—itching; Jagam ha—experienced. Rukmini Devi had never heard such unpleasant things from her beloved Trilokesh-Pati before.\nTherefore, she became frightened. A shiver started in her heart, and in intense 