      "textEn": "9\n\nTatasthavadhomchyatiduhkharudvak. 2. Meaning of the word\n\nShabdartha\nPada—with one's own feet; Su-jaten—extremely gentle; Nakh—nails; Arun—red; Shriya—sharp; Bhuvam—earth\nKo; Likhanti—scratching; Ashrubhih—with one's own tears; Anjan—kohl; Asitaih—black; Asinchati—being watered;\nKunkum—with kumkum powder; Rushitau—red; Stanaau—both breasts; Tasthau—became still; Adhah—downwards; Mukhi—\nMukhiye; Ati—extremely; Dukkha—with sorrow; Ruddha—stopped; Vak—speech..\nShe began to dig the ground with her soft feet, adorned with red, shiny nails, and\ndrowned the breasts, which had turned red from the kumkum, with the tears that had darkened her eyes. She stood with her face down, and with excessive sorrow, her speech was blocked.\nIt was. By that, the wise one, free from sorrow, fear, and grief,\nIs not afflicted by the twin afflictions of loss and ruin.\nThe body is agitated, and he is overwhelmed with sleep.\nLike a mountain, the wind is blowing, and he is scattered. 24). Meaning of the word\n\nTashyah - of him; Su-dukkha - excessive sorrow; Bhaya - fear; Shoka - and from sorrow; Vinashta - destroyed; Buddheah - with intellect;\nHastat - by hand; Shlathat - slipping; Valayat - with rings; Vyajanam - fan; Papta - fell; Dehah - his\nbody; Cha - also; Viklav - distressed; Dhiyah - whose mind; Sahasraav - suddenly; Muhyan - fainted; Rambha - banana tree;\nIva - like; Vayu - from the wind; Vihata - uprooted; Pravikiarya - scattered; Keshah - his hair..\nRukmini's mind was overwhelmed with sorrow, fear, and grief. Their bangles slipped from their hands,\nand their fan fell to the ground. She fainted out of delusion, her hair\nscattered here and there, and her body fell to the ground in such a way as a banana tree uprooted by the wind\nfalls.\nMeaning: Rukmini, not understanding that Lord Krishna's words were deeply moving, did not understand that God\nwas only tormenting them, which is why these feelings of sorrow were seen in them, which Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti has called\nSattvic,\nfeelings that range from being stunned to the state of death. Tad drishtva bhagavan krishnah priyaya premabandhamm. The laughter-filled, the compassionate, the one who trembles. 25. Lexicon"
